 #edb/ref 17592186368182 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186368175 #edb/ref 1069 "q-336900" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186368175 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186368175 #edb/ref 1071 "$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t)={x}^{2}(t)+2y(t)-6 \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)=x(t)-3y(t)+{y}^{2}(t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsider the system of differential equations above. What is the corresponding linearized system at the equilibrium point ${x}^{∗}=[2,\\text{ }1]^{T}?$\n\n| ${u}^{′}=\\,\\,$ |  | {{field-1}} | {{field-2}} |  | $\\,\\,u,\\,$ where $u=x-{x}^{∗}$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n|  | {{field-3}} | {{field-4}} |  |  |  |" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186368175 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "396d0927-5f69-505a-80d1-a119800f214e" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186368175 #edb/ref 1074 "The system can be rewritten in matrix form as \n\n$$\n{x}^{′}=\\text{\\boldsymbol}f(x)\n$$\n\nwhere $x=\\begin{bmatrix}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{bmatrix}$ and \n$\\text{\\boldsymbol}f(x)=\\begin{bmatrix}{x}^{2}+2y-6 \\\\ x-3y+{y}^{2}\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\nWe need to compute the Jacobian matrix (or the total derivative) of $\\text{\\boldsymbol}f(x).$ \n\nIn our case, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\text{\\boldsymbol}{f}^{′}(x)=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂x} & \\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂y} \\\\ \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂x} & \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂y}\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nFirst, we compute the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}+2y-6)=2x,\\, & \\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{2}+2y-6)=2 \\\\ \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(x-3y+{y}^{2})=1,\\, & \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(x-3y+{y}^{2})=-3+2y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, we have\n\n$$\n\\text{\\boldsymbol}{f}^{′}(x)=\\begin{bmatrix}2x & 2 \\\\ 1 & 2y-3\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nNext, we evaluate the Jacobian at the equilibrium ${x}^{∗}=\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}$ to get\n\n$$\n\\text{\\boldsymbol}{f}^{′}({x}^{∗})=\\begin{bmatrix}2(2) & 2 \\\\ 1 & 2(1)-3\\