}\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\nWe need to compute the Jacobian matrix (or the total derivative) of $\\text{\\boldsymbol}f(x).$ \n\nIn our case, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\text{\\boldsymbol}{f}^{′}(x)=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂x} & \\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂y} \\\\ \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂x} & \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂y}\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nFirst, we compute the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂x} & =2+3{x}^{2},\\, & \\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂y} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂x} & =-2,\\, & \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂y} & =3{y}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, we have\n\n$$\n\\text{\\boldsymbol}{f}^{′}(x)=\\begin{bmatrix}2+3{x}^{2} & 1 \\\\ -2 & 3{y}^{2}\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nNext, we evaluate the Jacobian at the equilibrium ${x}^{∗}=[0,\\text{ }0]^{T}$ to get\n\n$$\n\\text{\\boldsymbol}{f}^{′}({x}^{∗})=\\begin{bmatrix}2+0 & 1 \\\\ -2 & 0\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 1 \\\\ -2 & 0\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nTo find the eigenvalues of the Jacobian matrix, we write down the characteristic equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{vmatrix}2-\\lambda  & 1 \\\\ -2 & -\\lambda \\end{vmatrix} & =0 \\\\ (2-\\lambda )(-\\lambda )+2 & =0 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{2}-2\\lambda +2 & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving the equation, we get the eigenvalues ${\\lambda }_{1,2}=1\\pm i.$\n\nSince we have two complex eigenvalues with positive real parts, our equilibrium is an $\\text{unstable}$ $\\text{spiral source}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186368202 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186368203 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186368202 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186368204 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186368202 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045494 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186368202 #edb/ref 1077 "field-1" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186368202 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186368203 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186368202 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "7ee66c07-aedb-5dc2-9782-17c5870e5ecd" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186368203 #edb/ref 1080 "unstable" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186368203 #edb/ref 1